The advantage and beauty of script solutions is that you can rely on
existing (Unix-)tools, that the solution is very compact (just a few
lines compared to hundreds of C code lines), and adapt it quickly on
changing requirements.

I'll go and try a quick (just rudimentary tested) script enhancement
to consider the combinations you mention...

You need an additional filter (the awk-line in the middle):

cat record1 record2 ... recordN |
   awk 'x=$0, sub(","," ") {if (++c[$12,$13,$16] <= 3) print x}' |
     sort -nr +1 | head -n 100  > record.new

(The awk code seems a bit awkward (what a pun!) but is necessary due
to the inhomogeneous field separators in the record file.[*])

The above code considers (profession, race, name)-combinations and
a filter limit of 3 entries per combination.

If you like to have other combinations considered just change the
list within the square brackets:
$11 for userid, $12 for profession, $13 for race, $16 for name.

Use the scripts on your own risk. Enjoy!  8-)

